    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d283">

                <pb facs="0967" n="943" sameAs="frus:frus1946v02/pg943"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 EW (Peace)/9–2546</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The British Secretary of State for Foreign
                        Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Bevin</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>?,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-09-25">25 September, 1946</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear James</hi>: On 3rd September Dr. Wang Shih-Chieh
                    wrote me a letter explaining the attitude of the Chinese Government towards the
                    application of the Berlin and Moscow Agreements in relation to the conclusion of
                    a Peace Settlement with Germany. I understand that a similar communication has
                    been received by you.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d283-f1"/></p>
                <p>To my mind, Dr. Wang’s remarks seem to be entirely in conformity with the
                    decisions taken at Potsdam, but I have not wished to reply to his letter before
                    ascertaining whether my colleagues on the Council share my views. The attached
                    draft letter<anchor n="4" xml:id="d283-f2"/> has been prepared in the hope that
                    it may suggest the basis of the reply which we should each address to Dr. Wang
                    in this matter. I should be grateful if you would kindly let me know whether you
                    are in agreement with the terms of this draft or whether you have any comments
                    to make.</p>
                <p>Yours sincerely,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Ernest Bevin</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
